<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Javanshir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 637 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Juansher</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Javanshir was the most famous prince of Caucasian Albania, of the Mihranid family, ruling from about 637 until 680. After serving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, he led Albania on its own course, allying with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, fighting off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> raids and in 667 submitting to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Mu'awiya on favourable terms. He was murdered in a conspiracy in 680, and the History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> praises him at length.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/javanshir.jpg" alt="Javanshir" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_sculpture_of_Juansher.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juansher was the second of the four sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varaz-grigor">Varaz Grigor</a>, prince of Albania from 628 to 637, whom he succeeded. His family, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihranid-dynasty">Mihranids</a>, were of Parthian origin but claimed descent from the Sasanian kings; their ancestor Mihran was said to have received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gardman">Gardman</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a>, and around 600 a Mihranid prince conquered all of Albania and took the title Arranshah. The name Juansher comes from the Persian for young lion. He probably spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> and Armenian and knew Albanian, some Greek and perhaps a little Arabic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Juansher led the Albanian contingent in the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rostam Farrokhzad</a> against the Arab invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. He was badly wounded at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-al-qadisiyyah">Battle of al-Qadisiyyah</a> and escaped by swimming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, and later commanded 3,000 men guarding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a>'s withdrawal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>. In 644/45, losing faith in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>, he returned home and rebelled. The Sasanians retook the lowlands and the capital, Partaw, but his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war, backed by leading men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, forced them to grant him wide autonomy.</p><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> in 651 his father acknowledged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> but had Juansher offer his services to the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Constans II</a>, who made him protopatrikios and sent him a piece of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a>. This allegiance took effect during the caliphate's civil war of 656–661, when Constans anointed him king of all the eastern peoples in 661. Juansher made peace with the North Caucasian Huns after their raid of 665 and married their king's daughter.</p><p>He then submitted to the Umayyad caliph Mu'awiya I and, by report, advised him on how to kill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Constans II</a>, who was assassinated on 15 July 669. Juansher was rewarded with Siwnik and a third of the tribute the caliphate drew from Albania. He was murdered at Partaw during the Feast of the Cross by a traitor named Varaznoy; the historian James Howard-Johnston thinks he had lost the caliph's trust. His nephew Varaz-Tiridates I succeeded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Although a Christian, Juansher kept the Zoroastrian New Year feast of Nawasard and enjoyed the performances of the gusank, minstrels. A chapter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movses-kaghankatvatsi">Movses Kaghankatvatsi</a>'s History of the Caucasian Albanians contains a poem to him by Davtak Kertogh, counted the first long secular poem in Armenian literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juansher” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Nader Shah's death in 1747, Panah Ali Khan of the <b>Javanshir</b> tribe returned from exile and made himself khan of Karabakh.</blockquote><a href="/state/karabakh-khanate">Karabakh Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Javanshir</b>, the best-known prince, fought the Arabs, allied with Byzantium and then submitted to the caliphate in 667, while repelling Khazar raids.</blockquote><a href="/state/mihranid-principality-of-albania">Mihranid Principality of Albania</a></li></ul></section>
